**`@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")`** — creates synthetic `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken`:
- User doesn't need to exist in any `UserDetailsService`
- Created in memory by the test framework
- Use for testing authorization rules

```java
@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
void adminCanAccessUserList() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/users"))
        .andExpect(status().isOk());
}

@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
void userCannotAccessAdminEndpoint() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/users"))
        .andExpect(status().isForbidden());
}
```

**`@WithUserDetails("admin@test.com")`** — calls real `UserDetailsService`:
- Tests full authentication data path
- Custom `GrantedAuthority` mapping
- Account status flags (`isEnabled`, `isAccountNonLocked`)
- Custom `UserDetails` properties that authorization depends on
- If `@PreAuthorize` uses custom principal properties (org ID, subscription tier), `@WithUserDetails` loads real object

**Limitation:**
- `@WithUserDetails` requires user to exist in DB or mock repository
- For precise control without DB dependency, prefer `@WithMockUser` or custom annotations

**Key difference:**
- `@WithMockUser` -> `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken`
- `@WithUserDetails` -> calls `loadUserByUsername()` and creates token from returned `UserDetails`
- If your authz code does `instanceof` check on principal, the difference matters

**Problem:** 50 controller tests repeat `@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")`. Admin role requirements change -> update one line, miss 49 others.

**Custom meta-annotations solve this:**

```java
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@WithMockUser(username = "admin@test.com", roles = {"ADMIN", "USER"})
public @interface WithMockAdmin {
}

@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@WithMockUser(username = "user@test.com", roles = {"USER"})
public @interface WithMockRegularUser {
}
```

**Usage cleaner and immediately legible:**

```java
@Test
@WithMockAdmin
void adminCanDeleteUser() throws Exception { ... }

@Test
@WithMockRegularUser
void regularUserCannotDeleteUser() throws Exception { ... }
```

**For JWT-based apps — custom `@WithSecurityContext`:**

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@WithSecurityContext(factory = WithMockJwtUserSecurityContextFactory.class)
public @interface WithMockJwtUser {
    String subject() default "user123";
    String[] roles() default {"USER"};
    String orgId() default "org-default";
}

public class WithMockJwtUserSecurityContextFactory
        implements WithSecurityContextFactory<WithMockJwtUser> {
    @Override
    public SecurityContext createSecurityContext(WithMockJwtUser ann) {
        Jwt jwt = Jwt.withTokenValue("mock-token")
            .header("alg", "none")
            .subject(ann.subject())
            .claim("roles", List.of(ann.roles()))
            .claim("org_id", ann.orgId())
            .build();
        Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = Arrays.stream(ann.roles())
            .map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + r))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        Authentication auth = new JwtAuthenticationToken(jwt, authorities);
        SecurityContext ctx = SecurityContextHolder.createEmptyContext();
        ctx.setAuthentication(auth);
        return ctx;
    }
}
```

**Test coverage goal:**
- For every `requestMatchers` rule and every `@PreAuthorize`, two tests:
  - One succeeds with required role
  - One fails without it
- Untested authorization rules = security gaps in production

**Method security context requirements:**
- Spring context must load security configuration
- `@WithMockUser` works with `@SpringBootTest` or `@WebMvcTest`
- **Without `@EnableMethodSecurity` in the loaded context, `@PreAuthorize` annotations are ignored and method security tests always pass** — the most insidious false positive

**Minimal Spring test for method security:**

```java
@SpringBootTest(classes = {OrderService.class, MethodSecurityConfig.class})
@WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
class OrderServiceSecurityTest {
    @Autowired OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void adminOnlyMethod_userRole_shouldThrowAccessDenied() {
        assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class, () -> orderService.deleteAll());
    }
}
```

**Useful security result matchers:**
- `SecurityMockMvcResultMatchers.authenticated()` — assert authenticated
- `.unauthenticated()` — assert no auth
- `.withAuthorities("ROLE_ADMIN")` — assert specific authorities
- These confirm `SecurityContext`, not just HTTP response
- Useful when 200 could come from authenticated access OR misconfigured `permitAll()`

**Things `@WithMockUser` does NOT test:**
- The actual login flow (form submission, JWT issuance)
- `UserDetailsService` lookups (use `@WithUserDetails`)
- Password encoding/verification (test directly on `PasswordEncoder`)
- Filter chain ordering (use real integration tests with `@SpringBootTest` + `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`)
- OAuth2/OIDC flows (use Keycloak Testcontainers or WireMock for IdP simulation)

**Standard slice test for a single controller with security:**

```java
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
@Import(SecurityConfig.class)
class OrderControllerSecurityTest {

    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void anonymousIsUnauthorized() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/orders"))
            .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void userCanListOrders() throws Exception {
        when(orderService.list(any())).thenReturn(List.of(new Order("o-1")));
        mockMvc.perform(get("/orders"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$[0].id").value("o-1"));
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void userCannotDelete() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(delete("/orders/o-1").with(csrf()))
            .andExpect(status().isForbidden());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
    void adminCanDelete() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(delete("/orders/o-1").with(csrf()))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
        verify(orderService).delete("o-1");
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
    void deleteWithoutCsrfIsForbidden() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(delete("/orders/o-1"))
            .andExpect(status().isForbidden());
    }
}
```

**What the setup gives you:**
- `@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)` — only that controller and MVC infra
- `@Import(SecurityConfig.class)` — your `SecurityFilterChain` bean
- `@MockBean OrderService` — replaces the real service so logic is isolated
- `MockMvc` is auto-built with `springSecurity()` applied — no manual setup

**Result matcher for security context (assert beyond HTTP status):**

```java
import static org.springframework.security.test.web.servlet.response.SecurityMockMvcResultMatchers.*;

@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
void userIsAuthenticatedWithExpectedAuthority() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(get("/orders"))
        .andExpect(authenticated().withAuthorities(
            List.of(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_USER"))));
}
```

**`@PreAuthorize` requires AOP proxies — only created in `@SpringBootTest`:**

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public void deleteAll() { ... }

    @PreAuthorize("#order.userId == authentication.name")
    public Order get(Order order) { return order; }
}

@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity
public class MethodSecurityConfig {}
```

**Test that exercises the method-security proxy:**

```java
@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceMethodSecurityTest {

    @Autowired OrderService service;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void userCannotDeleteAll() {
        assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class, () -> service.deleteAll());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
    void adminCanDeleteAll() {
        assertDoesNotThrow(() -> service.deleteAll());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(username = "alice")
    void aliceCanGetHerOwnOrder() {
        Order own = new Order("o-1", "alice");
        assertEquals("o-1", service.get(own).id());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(username = "alice")
    void aliceCannotGetBobsOrder() {
        Order bobs = new Order("o-2", "bob");
        assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class, () -> service.get(bobs));
    }
}
```

**Why `@WebMvcTest` cannot test this:**
- `@WebMvcTest` loads only the MVC layer
- AOP proxies for `@PreAuthorize` are NOT created
- Calling `service.deleteAll()` directly bypasses the annotation
- Tests pass even when `@PreAuthorize` is broken — silent false positive

**The check that catches the false positive:**

```java
@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
void methodSecurityIsActuallyActive() {
    // If @EnableMethodSecurity is missing, this test passes incorrectly
    assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class, () -> service.deleteAll());
}
```

**Run this test in CI** as a canary — if it ever stops failing for a non-admin, method security has been disabled.

**JWT Resource Server endpoints need `JwtAuthenticationToken`. `@WithMockUser` creates the wrong type. Use `jwt()` post-processor or build a custom annotation for reuse.**

**Custom annotation:**

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@WithSecurityContext(factory = WithMockJwtUserSecurityContextFactory.class)
public @interface WithMockJwtUser {
    String subject() default "user-123";
    String[] roles() default {"USER"};
    String orgId() default "org-default";
}
```

**Factory that builds the `SecurityContext`:**

```java
public class WithMockJwtUserSecurityContextFactory
        implements WithSecurityContextFactory<WithMockJwtUser> {

    @Override
    public SecurityContext createSecurityContext(WithMockJwtUser ann) {
        Jwt jwt = Jwt.withTokenValue("mock-token")
            .header("alg", "none")
            .subject(ann.subject())
            .claim("roles", List.of(ann.roles()))
            .claim("org_id", ann.orgId())
            .build();
        Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = Arrays.stream(ann.roles())
            .map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + r))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        SecurityContext ctx = SecurityContextHolder.createEmptyContext();
        ctx.setAuthentication(new JwtAuthenticationToken(jwt, authorities));
        return ctx;
    }
}
```

**Usage:**

```java
@Test
@WithMockJwtUser(subject = "alice", roles = {"ADMIN"}, orgId = "acme")
void adminFromAcmeCanDelete() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/orders/o-1").with(csrf()))
        .andExpect(status().isOk());
}
```

**For per-request precision (different JWT per request in same test):**

```java
mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/o-1")
    .with(jwt()
        .jwt(jb -> jb.subject("alice").claim("org_id", "acme"))
        .authorities(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_USER"))))
    .andExpect(status().isOk());
```

**For OAuth2 login flows:**

```java
mockMvc.perform(get("/profile")
    .with(oauth2Login()
        .attributes(a -> a.put("email", "alice@example.com"))
        .authorities(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_USER"))))
    .andExpect(status().isOk());
```

**For the highest fidelity — real OAuth2/OIDC flows — use Keycloak Testcontainers.**

**Dependency:**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.github.dasniko</groupId>
    <artifactId>testcontainers-keycloak</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

**Test setup:**

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)
@Testcontainers
class OAuth2ResourceServerIntegrationTest {

    @Container
    static KeycloakContainer keycloak = new KeycloakContainer()
        .withRealmImportFile("realm-export.json");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void registerProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri",
            () -> keycloak.getAuthServerUrl() + "/realms/test");
    }

    @LocalServerPort int port;
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate rest;

    @Test
    void unauthorizedRequestIs401() {
        ResponseEntity<String> response = rest.getForEntity(
            "http://localhost:" + port + "/api/orders", String.class);
        assertEquals(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED, response.getStatusCode());
    }

    @Test
    void validJwtIsAccepted() {
        String token = obtainTokenFromKeycloak("alice", "password");
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.setBearerAuth(token);
        ResponseEntity<String> response = rest.exchange(
            "http://localhost:" + port + "/api/orders",
            HttpMethod.GET, new HttpEntity<>(headers), String.class);
        assertEquals(HttpStatus.OK, response.getStatusCode());
    }
}
```

**Why this level of testing matters:**
- Real JWKS endpoint, real key rotation behavior
- Real `iss`/`aud` validation
- Real signature verification
- Catches issues that mocked tokens cannot reveal

**Cost:**
- Slower than `@WebMvcTest` (Keycloak container startup)
- Use sparingly — one or two tests per critical flow, not every endpoint
